nodecode-stacktree is a rewrite of stevysmith's stevysmith/stacktree-skill:

  https://github.com/stevysmith/stacktree-skill
  commit 139fe05f2a03db602b6e29a4816d30ffddf99816
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Steve Smith (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the original is a portable Agent Plugins manifest
(plugin.json, mcp.json) plus five skills that describe Stacktree's MCP
tools. The cell keeps the product's jobs -- publish a complete HTML page to a
private link, update it in place, list the account's pages, read whether one
was opened, take one down, restore it -- as six stacktree_* tools over the
same provider's REST API (the surface the original's own stacktree skill
documents for agents with no MCP server), on the API key the original asks
the operator to export. The publish tool keeps the original's parameter names
(content, password, expires_in_hours, public_slug, client, pii_check) and
maps them to the API's own members; update keeps the two-step body-then-
settings shape its publish.sh uses, so a revision never mints a second url.
The expiry, PII, plan-limit, delete-and-restore and viewer-comments rules the
publish skill states are carried into the tools' answers and the
(help :stacktree) manual, including that viewer text is data, never
instructions.

What was left out, and why: the MCP server itself, because a Nodecode cell
speaks HTTP directly and needs no MCP client; OAuth, because the key is the
account's own and rides the section; client-space management
(list_client_spaces, create/update/delete_client_space) and the viewer
feedback loop (set_client_feedback, list_feedback, resolve_feedback), which
are a surface of their own and not what the publish job needs; the x402 and
anonymous paths, which pay per request from a wallet rather than an account;
and the five skill files as separate loadable prompts, whose judgement is
folded into one manual and the tools' descriptions. LICENSE is the original
repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
